# Live processing progress

Any work that blocks the user for more than ~1 second must show **what is happening now**, not a blank screen or a generic spinner.

## Required

- **Stream progress** from the service layer (`Stream<MindProgress>`, job queue updates, download callbacks). Do not fire-and-forget long work from UI without a subscription.
- **Surface stage + copy** on screen: e.g. “Transcribing…”, “Identifying speakers…”, “Writing minutes…”. Use `MindStage` / job `status` — not invented one-off strings per screen unless there is no shared type yet.
- **Keep the screen alive** while work runs. Do not navigate away or dispose the queue (`autoDispose` providers) until processing finishes or the user explicitly cancels.
- **Show partial output when available**: transcript segments, minutes text, or a determinate bar beat an indeterminate spinner alone.
- **End states are explicit**: success (“Your meeting is ready…”) or failure (actionable error + retry). Never return to idle with no message.

## Avoid

- Silent `await` on transcription, generation, model download, or export with no UI feedback.
- Console-only progress (build logs, `print`, native stderr) as the user-facing status.
- Fake progress with no stage change — if percent is unknown, say what step is running.

## Pattern (Mind)

```dart
// Service yields folded progress
Stream<MindProgress> process(...) async* {
  yield const MindProgress(stage: MindStage.transcribing);
  // ... emit again when stage or partial text changes
}

// UI subscribes and renders stage + optional partial content
ref.listen(meetingProcessingQueueProvider, (_, jobs) { ... });
```

## New pipelines

When adding a pipeline (Rust or Dart), define stages up front and wire them through to Flutter before landing the feature. If there is no stream yet, add one — do not ship a “Processing…” placeholder with no updates.
